(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-adrenergic"]={"title":"Adrenergic & Blockers Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which decongestant causes rebound congestion — rhinitis medicamentosa — if used for more than three days?","choices":["Oxymetazoline","Pseudoephedrine","Phenylephrine","Albuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rhinitis medicamentosa beyond THREE DAYS. That three-day limit is the patient-education point. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which drug induces REFLEX BRADYCARDIA?","choices":["Isoproterenol","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine","Epinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pure alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises blood pressure, and the baroreceptor answer is a reflex slowing of the heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Extravasation causing blanching and sloughing of the skin belongs to which drug?","choices":["Dopamine","Dobutamine","Norepinephrine","Isoproterenol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Extravasation blanches then sloughs the skin, and it is treated with the alpha antagonist phentolamine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which drug is given to prevent dermal necrosis after norepinephrine extravasation?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Propranolol","Prazosin","Phentolamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An alpha antagonist, infiltrated locally. It is also used for short-term management of phaeochromocytoma and for hypertensive crisis after abrupt clonidine withdrawal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"“First-dose” syncope is the warning attached to which class?","choices":["Alpha-1 blockers","Beta-1 selective blockers","Beta-2 agonists","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. First-dose syncope with orthostatic hypotension — which is why the first dose is taken at bedtime. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which alpha-1 blockers are used specifically for benign prostatic hyperplasia?","choices":["Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Phentolamine","Labetalol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The uroselective ones — the rest of the class is used for blood pressure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which beta blocker causes arrhythmias if it is stopped abruptly?","choices":["Phentolamine","Prazosin","Propranolol","Albuterol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt withdrawal causes rebound arrhythmia — the class must be tapered. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Fasting hypoglycaemia with raised low-density lipoprotein and triglycerides belongs to which drug?","choices":["Atenolol","Clonidine","Prazosin","Propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic effects of nonselective beta blockade, alongside sexual impairment and central effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which drug causes REBOUND HYPERTENSION if stopped abruptly?","choices":["Clonidine","Propranolol","Prazosin","Tamsulosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rebound hypertension on abrupt discontinuation — and phentolamine is used if a crisis follows. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which drug is used to minimise withdrawal from opiates, tobacco and benzodiazepines?","choices":["Propranolol","Clonidine","Phenoxybenzamine","Amphetamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A central alpha-2 agonist, used to blunt withdrawal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is given intravenously for a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Labetalol","Timolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous labetalol for hypertensive emergencies; it and carvedilol both cause orthostatic hypotension through alpha-1 blockade. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which beta blocker prevents cardiovascular mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Propranolol","Esmolol","Timolol","Carvedilol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Named for the mortality benefit in heart failure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which beta blocker reduces production of aqueous humour in chronic open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["Timolol","Propranolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It reduces aqueous production — a different mechanism from pilocarpine, which improves outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Cardioselectivity is LOST under what circumstance?","choices":["In renal impairment","At higher doses","When given intravenously","In elderly patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-1 selective agents stop being selective as the dose rises, which matters in a patient with airway disease. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which class is useful for hypertension in a patient with impaired pulmonary function?","choices":["Nonselective beta blockers","Cholinergic agonists","Beta-1 selective blockers","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol and esmolol — selective, so the airway is spared. But only at lower doses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which drug is used for cardiogenic and septic shock, acting as a beta-1 inotrope with alpha-1 vasoconstriction only at very high doses?","choices":["Dobutamine","Phenylephrine","Isoproterenol","Dopamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dose-response is the whole point: beta-1 first, alpha-1 only at the top of the range. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which drug increases atrioventricular conduction, so it needs caution in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Dobutamine","Phenylephrine","Clonidine","Propranolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased AV conduction is the caution — in atrial fibrillation it can speed the ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Cerebral haemorrhage from a sharp rise in blood pressure is a risk with which drug?","choices":["Albuterol","Epinephrine","Dobutamine","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alongside anxiety, tremor, headache, arrhythmias and pulmonary oedema. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which drug is used to stimulate the heart in an emergency and is a pure beta agonist?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Norepinephrine","Isoproterenol","Clonidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nonselective beta, so it drives rate and contractility with no alpha vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Tremor, restlessness, apprehension and anxiety are the side effects of which drug?","choices":["Ipratropium","Propranolol","Atropine","Albuterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonist effects, and the reason a patient feels shaky after a rescue inhaler. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which alpha blocker is IRREVERSIBLE, so its effect lasts at least twenty-four hours?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Phentolamine","Prazosin","Tamsulosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible blockade lasting at least 24 hours, used in phaeochromocytoma, and it produces epinephrine reversal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which drug is used for hyperactivity, narcolepsy and appetite control?","choices":["Clonidine","Amphetamine","Cocaine","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three indications, through raised blood pressure, cardiac stimulation and central activity. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which sympathomimetic has a legitimate use as a LOCAL ANAESTHETIC?","choices":["Amphetamine","Ephedrine","Cocaine","Tyramine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Its one therapeutic use. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Ephedra-containing herbal products were banned by the Food and Drug Administration in which year?","choices":["1994","2010","2016","2004"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Banned in 2004; pseudoephedrine remains available for nasal and sinus congestion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Poor penetration into the central nervous system is a property of which class?","choices":["Catecholamines","Amphetamines","Alpha-1 blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoproterenol and dopamine — they do not cross well, so their effects are peripheral. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which drug produces orthostatic hypotension because it blocks alpha-1 in addition to beta receptors?","choices":["Atenolol","Labetalol","Propranolol","Timolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mixed alpha and beta blockade, which is where the orthostatic drop comes from. Carvedilol shares this. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which agent is used for the SHORT-TERM management of phaeochromocytoma?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Propranolol","Phentolamine","Clonidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Short-term; phenoxybenzamine is the irreversible one used for longer preparation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which drug relieves nasal and sinus congestion and is taken by mouth?","choices":["Oxymetazoline","Albuterol","Isoproterenol","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral decongestant — unlike oxymetazoline, which is the topical one with the three-day rebound limit. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias are listed for which drug?","choices":["Dopamine","Albuterol","Clonidine","Prazosin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its side effects, used in cardiogenic and septic shock. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Dizziness, lack of energy, nasal congestion and drowsiness point to which class?","choices":["Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers","Antimuscarinics","Cholinergic agonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 blockade opens the nasal vessels and drops the blood pressure — hence congestion plus dizziness. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"}]};
